**assistant**:
> Yes—mainly **configuration, composability, and testability**, rather than any intrinsic advantage of classes.
> 
> Your current module works well as a singleton adapter, but a class lets you create independent configured instances:
> 
> ```python
> adapter = OpenAIAdapter(
>     client=client,
>     provider_model_name="gpt-5.4-nano",
>     stream_model_name="gpt-5.6-terra",
>     temperature=1,
>     max_completion_tokens=3000,
> )
> ```
> 
> That gains you:
> 
> - **Multiple models/providers simultaneously** without globals or duplicate modules.
> - **Planner registration by instance**, e.g. distinct cheap/fast/high-quality model adapters.
> - **Per-adapter accounting and health state** instead of global `token_in_count` / `token_out_count`.
> - **Easy dependency injection in tests**: pass a fake OpenAI client.
> - **Explicit immutable-ish configuration**, avoiding hidden module state from `.env`, global client, and hard-coded models.
> - **A stable protocol**: every provider can expose the same adapter object methods, even when its SDK differs.
> 
> A module is perfectly valid if you only ever want one OpenAI configuration. Python modules are effectively singleton objects, and your current imported `stream_response` is simple and ergonomic.
> 
> But it becomes awkward when you want, for example:
> 
> ```python
> planner.register_model(
>     OpenAIAdapter(client, "gpt-5.4-nano"),
>     model_id="cheap",
> )
> 
> planner.register_model(
>     OpenAIAdapter(client, "gpt-5.6-terra", verbosity="low"),
>     model_id="capable",
> )
> ```
> 
> I would make it a small class—probably a `@dataclass`—but retain convenience wrappers if existing callers import the module functions:
> 
> ```python
> _default = OpenAIAdapter.from_env(model="gpt-5.4-nano")
> 
> def stream_response(messages, max_tokens=None):
>     return _default.stream_response(messages, max_tokens)
> ```
> 
> One architectural correction: `chat_response` and `stream_response` currently silently use different models. That can be intentional, but it should be explicit in the adapter’s configuration or represented as two registered adapters, rather than hidden behavior.
